'''Dutch art''' describes the history of [[visual arts]] in the [[Netherlands]], after the [[Dutch Republic|United Provinces]] separated from [[Flanders]]. Earlier painting in the area is covered in [[Early Netherlandish painting]] and [[Dutch and Flemish Renaissance painting]].
{{History of Dutch and Flemish painting}}
Dutch Golden Age painting, spanning from about 1620 to 1680, was a distinct style and movement that evolved out of the [[Flemish Baroque painting|Flemish Baroque tradition]]. It was a period of great artistic achievement in the Netherlands.  There was a healthy artistic climate in Dutch cities during the seventeenth century. For example, between 1605 and 1635, over 100,000 paintings were produced in [[Haarlem]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.franshalsmuseum.nl/tentoonstelling/archief/3/haarlem-the-cradle-of-the-golden-age |title=Haarlem: The Cradle of the Golden Age |access-date=15 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110727061426/http://www.franshalsmuseum.nl/tentoonstelling/archief/3/haarlem-the-cradle-of-the-golden-age |archive-date=27 July 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref> At that time, art ownership in the city was 25%, a record high.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.franshalsmuseum.nl/tentoonstelling/archief/3/haarlem-the-cradle-of-the-golden-age |title=Haarlem: The Cradle of the Golden Age |access-date=15 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110727061426/http://www.franshalsmuseum.nl/tentoonstelling/archief/3/haarlem-the-cradle-of-the-golden-age |archive-date=27 July 2011 |url-status=dead }}, see also [[Frans Hals Museum]]</ref>  After the end of the Golden Age, production of paintings remained high, but ceased to influence the rest of Europe as strongly.

Many painters, sculptors and architects of the seventeenth century are called "[[Dutch masters|Dutch]] [[Old Master|masters]]", while earlier artists are generally referred to as part of the "[[Netherlands (terminology)#Netherlandish|Netherlandish]]" tradition. When a work of art is labelled as 'Dutch School', it means that the specific artist who created it is unknown.

The [[Hague School]] of the 19th century re-interpreted the range of subjects of the Golden Age in contemporary terms, and made Dutch painting once again a European leader. In the successive movements of art since the 19th century, the Dutch contribution has been best known from the work of the individual figures of [[Vincent van Gogh]] and [[Piet Mondrian]], though both did their best work outside the Netherlands, and took some time to be appreciated. [[Amsterdam Impressionism]] had a mainly local impact, but the ''[[De Stijl]]'' movement, of which Mondrian was a member, was influential abroad.

