The '''Institute of Astronomy'''<ref name="NCU_structure_20191104" />{{rp|6}} of [[Nicolaus Copernicus University in Toruń]], known prior to 1 October 2019 in scientific publications as the '''Toruń Centre for Astronomy''',<ref name="Lancaster2011_TCfA" /> is an optical and [[radio]] [[observatory]] located at ({{coord|53|5|42.9|N|18|33|45.9|E}}) in [[Piwnice, Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship|Piwnice]], about 15&nbsp;km north of [[Toruń]], Poland. It houses two single-dish [[Antenna (radio)|antenna]] [[telescopes]], 32&nbsp;metres and 15&nbsp;metres in [[diameter]], as well as the largest Polish optical telescope – 90&nbsp;cm Schmidt-Cassegrain camera. The facility is operated by the [[Nicolaus Copernicus University]]. Also, photometry using 60&nbsp;cm Cassegrain telescope is made and radio measurements of the Sun at 127&nbsp;MHz frequency have been recorded on a daily basis since 1958 using a 23&nbsp;m interferometer.

{{Short description|Physics property associated with symmetries}}
{{About|charge in the most general physics sense|charge as relates to electrical phenomena|electric charge|other uses of charge|Charge (disambiguation){{!}}Charge}}

{{Refimprove|date=October 2015}}
In [[physics]], a '''charge''' is any of many different quantities, such as the [[electric charge]] in [[electromagnetism]] or the [[color charge]] in [[quantum chromodynamics]]. Charges correspond to the [[time-invariant]] [[generating set of a group|generators]] of a [[symmetry group]], and specifically, to the generators that [[commutator (physics)|commute with]] the [[Hamiltonian (quantum mechanics)|Hamiltonian]].  Charges are often denoted by {{tmath| Q }}, and so the invariance of the charge corresponds to the vanishing [[commutator]] {{tmath|1= [Q,H]=0 }}, where <math>H</math> is the Hamiltonian. Thus, charges are associated with conserved [[quantum number]]s; these are the eigenvalues of the generator <math>Q</math>. A "charge" can also refer to a point-shaped object with an electric charge and a position, such as in the [[method of image charges]].

{{Other uses|Frost line (disambiguation)}}
{{Short description|Distance from a young star where ice forms}}
In [[astronomy]] or [[planetary science]], the '''frost line''', also known as the '''snow line''' or '''ice line''', is the minimum distance from the central [[protostar]] of a [[protoplanetary disk|solar nebula]] where the [[temperature]] is low enough for [[volatile (astrogeology)|volatile]] compounds such as [[water]], [[ammonia]], [[methane]], [[carbon dioxide]] and [[carbon monoxide]] to [[condense]] into [[solid]] grains, which will allow their [[accretion (astrophysics)|accretion]] into [[planetesimal]]s. Beyond the line, otherwise [[gas]]eous compounds (which are much more abundant) can be quite easily condensed to allow formation of [[gas giant]]s and [[ice giant]]s; while within it, only heavier compounds can be accreted to form the typically much smaller [[rocky planet]]s.

The term itself is [[loanword|borrowed]] from the notion of "[[frost line]]" in [[soil science]], which describes the maximum depth from the surface that [[groundwater]] can freeze.

Each volatile substance has its own frost line (e.g., carbon monoxide,<ref>{{Cite journal|arxiv=1307.7439|title=Imaging of the CO Snow Line in a Solar Nebula Analog by Chunhua Qi, Karin I. Oberg, et al |journal=Science |volume=341 |issue=6146 |pages=630–2 |last1=Qi |first1=Chunhua |last2= Oberg |first2=Karin I. |last3= Wilner |first3=David J. |last4=d'Alessio |first4=Paola |last5=Bergin |first5=Edwin |last6= Andrews |first6=Sean M. |last7= Blake |first7=Geoffrey A. |last8= Hogerheijde |first8=Michiel R. |last9= van Dishoeck |first9=Ewine F. |year=2013 |doi=10.1126/science.1239560 |pmid=23868917|bibcode=2013Sci...341..630Q |s2cid=23271440 }}</ref> [[nitrogen]],<ref>{{cite journal |title=UltraCarbonaceous Antarctic micrometeorites, probing the Solar System beyond the nitrogen snow-line by E. Dartois, et al | doi=10.1016/j.icarus.2013.03.002 |bibcode=2013Icar..224..243D |volume=224 |issue=1 |journal=Icarus |pages=243–252|last1=Dartois |first1=E. |last2=Engrand |first2=C. |last3=Brunetto |first3=R. |last4=Duprat |first4=J. |last5=Pino |first5=T. |last6=Quirico |first6=E. |last7=Remusat |first7=L. |last8=Bardin |first8=N. |last9=Briani |first9=G. |last10=Mostefaoui |first10=S. |last11=Morinaud |first11=G. |last12=Crane |first12=B. |last13=Szwec |first13=N. |last14=Delauche |first14=L. |last15=Jamme |first15=F. |last16=Sandt |first16=Ch. |last17=Dumas |first17=P. |year=2013 }}</ref> and [[argon]]<ref name=n2_snowline_2019>{{cite journal | last1=Öberg | first1=K.I. | last2=Wordsworth | first2=R. | title=Jupiter's Composition Suggests its Core Assembled Exterior to the N_{2} Snowline | journal=The Astronomical Journal | year=2019 | volume=158 | issue=5 | doi=10.3847/1538-3881/ab46a8 | s2cid=202749962 | doi-access=free | arxiv=1909.11246 }}</ref>), so it is important to always specify which material's frost line is referred to, though omission is common, especially for the water frost line. A [[tracer gas]] may be used for materials that are otherwise difficult to detect; for example [[diazenylium]] for carbon monoxide.

